{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791065075,"nonce":"SG8w6mA1eHO1EMIEQjVBF2QKoLk=","title":"Inline text flow: text across inline elements, as one paragraph","message":"Built-in text elements (ea9cd53) are boxes: \u003cp\u003eHi \u003cstrong\u003ethere\u003c/strong\u003e and \u003ccode\u003ex\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e lays out its text runs and inline elements as separate boxes in a wrapping row. So a line cannot break inside one box and continue in the next, the spaces at the edges of each run collapse ('strongand' in tools/snapshot/scenes/Controls.hx), and inline boxes like code sit on their own baseline. HTML's inline formatting context needs the paragraph's runs shaped and wrapped together as one text with styled spans (weight, style, colour, background, font). Blinc has rich_text and styled_text in blinc_layout (rich_text/, styled_text.rs); a text node of spans through blinc_abi, with hxx lowering a text element's inline children into its spans, would do it. Inline elements then keep their identity for CSS by span.","files":null}]}